```cs
public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == true && New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
}
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
</code>

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0);
</code>

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_Ret || New_arrListIndexOfX == -1);
</code>

<code>
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret, "If the return value is true, index of x in new array is >= 0");
</code>
The main implementation of this task involves splitting the paragraph by paragraph into sentences and extracting phrases for each sentence that contain "@@@". Then use a regular expression to match and print out those phrases as assert statements. Here's a Python code snippet that could be used to achieve this:

```python
import re
debug_assert_pattern = r"@@@\s*(.+)\r"

def debug_assert_to_code(input_code):
    debug_asserts = re.findall(debug_assert_pattern, input_code)
    for assert_phrase in debug_asserts:
        input_code = input_code.replace("@@@ " + assert_phrase, f"Debug.Assert({assert_phrase},\"{assert_phrase}\");", 1)
    return input_code

# Usage:
input_code = """
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

	    // @@@ If the return value is true, index of x in new array is >= 0
        }
    }
}
"""
output_code = debug_assert_to_code(input_code)
print(output_code)
```
This code will match comments that start with "@@@" and transform them into Debug.Assert statements.

Note, you may need to install the regex package (through pip) using `pip install regex`.

```shell
pip install regex
```

Python code won't be able to parse C# code perfectly, as C# and Python syntax are quite different. C# code also does not directly support the usage of Debug.Assert statements in the same way as the Python debugging package. Thus, to parse C# code or generate C# code more appropriately you would still need a C# parser library.--------------------